  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 132 AM CDT Thu Sep 10 2026\n\nTODAY...It'll be a nice one! Mostly sunny skies combine with \nlight winds under surface high pressure today. Winds start to \nturn out of the south, however, bringing temperatures and \ndewpoints up slightly from yesterday.\n\nFRIDAY...The setup remains largely the same for Friday evening\nas a bonafide cold front moves southeast from northern MN, \nlikely triggering scattered storms along its path. If there's \nany change to the forecast, it is increasing confidence for \nnorthern and central MN, and decreasing confidence for southern \nMN. As the SPC highlights in this morning's SWODY2 discussion, \nweaker large scale ascent may become problematic for overcoming \nwarmer 850-700 mb temperatures to the south. We are seeing this\nmanifest with some of the hi-res CAMs that are starting to get \ninto range, with storms only getting as far south as a line \nfrom Morris to St Cloud. For those areas to the north, veering \nlow level winds and increasing surface moisture will aid in the \ndevelopment of initial supercells. This is where there is a \ngreater risk for large hail, damaging winds, and even a low \ntornado potential. A strengthening LLJ would favor storms \nbecoming more clustered through the evening, which would\nmitigate the severe risk the further south and east the front \ngets. Timing remains pretty consistent, with the initial storms \ndeveloping in the late afternoon or early evening, and \ndissipating by midnight or so.\n\nSATURDAY AND BEYOND...Temperatures behind the front will be much \nmore seasonable with highs in the low to mid 70s and dewpoints \nin the 50s. The Upper MS Valley will become sandwiched between \nthe exiting cold front and a developing low pressure system over\nthe PNW Saturday into Sunday. As the approaching low deepens \noff the lee of the Rockies, a pool of surface moisture is \nexpected to advect north across the region, rising PWAT \nanomalies to 150-200% of normal for mid September. In fact, any \nvalues approaching 1.8\" is near the 99th percentile of sounding \nclimatology for MPX this time of year. All of this moisture is \npointless though, unless there is enough forcing to do something\nwith it. There continues to be discrepancies between the \nevolution of the upper low, with it potentially lagging out west\nlonger. At the same time though, the LLJ increasing in strength\nSunday night would trigger and fuel widespread showers and \nstorms. As the upper low moves east, rain likely continues into \nthe first part of Monday.\n\nThe jet stream becomes more zonal, and eventually shifts out of\nthe northwest, for the rest of the week. This will bring in a \ncooler, drier air mass at least for few days, but potentially \neven longer. One interesting thing to note is that we could see \nour first high in the 60s since June 17th at MSP. (I know, kind\nof odd for it to be June... it was a day that was very cloudy \nand rainy.) We do have PoPs return Wednesday evening, indicating\nthe chance for some shortwaves to move southeast along the \nflow.",
